const bodyContent = `<p>On 11 November United Airlines put out <a href="https://united.mediaroom.com/news-releases?item=125496">a press release titled "United Restricts Access for Third-Party AI Agents"</a> and changed its contract of carriage to match. The reasoning is about accounts: the airline wrote that "sharing account credentials with personal, third-party, AI assistants can expose sensitive information", and that actions taken by those tools "may result in unintended purchases, account changes, or use of MileagePlus miles." A lot of what people ask a travel assistant needs no account at all, though: where the inbound aircraft is, what route a callsign flies, what the weather is doing at the other end. This post walks through live flight data for an AI agent from a Pilot Cloud node that never holds an airline login, including the parts that broke when we ran it on 13 November.</p><section><h2>What United restricted, in its own words</h2><p>United says it will restrict access "directly within our digital platforms and indirectly via travel agencies and aggregators", and it adds a safety argument that has nothing to do with passwords: "flight check-in requires human acknowledgment that bags do not contain banned substances like explosives."</p><p>The contract wording has been quoted by <a href="https://simpleflying.com/united-airlines-bans-ai-agents-booking-flights-check-in/">Simple Flying</a>, which puts it in rule 6.J.6: "the use of AI Agents to make, modify or service a Ticket, and/or to purchase any service or product." <a href="https://onemileatatime.com/news/united-airlines-bans-ai-agents-protect-customers/">Ben Schlappig at One Mile at a Time</a> quotes the full definition: software that acts "whether or not Passenger or another individual supervises or approves its actions" to "discover, search for, book, purchase, ticket, service or manage" anything United sells. He reads that as broad enough to catch award search tools.</p><p>We are not offering a reading of that contract, and whether a given tool falls inside it is a question for United and your own counsel. The point here is narrower: everything below comes from aircraft broadcasts and public data services, and no step touches an airline account, a booking or a check-in.</p></section><section><h2>A read-only connection to the node</h2><p>A Pilot Cloud node is one URL in an MCP client. <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/mcp">The Pilot Cloud reference</a> lists two OAuth scopes: pilot, which covers everything, and pilot.read, where a connection "sees and runs only the read-only tools". The server's 401 challenge names scope="pilot", so a client that just follows it asks for full access. An agent that only watches flights has no use for that. Claude Code can <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp#restrict-oauth-scopes">pin the scopes it requests</a> with oauth.scopes, a space-separated string in the format of <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749#section-3.3">RFC 6749 section 3.3</a>:</p><pre><code>{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pilot": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp",
      "oauth": { "scopes": "pilot.read" }
    }
  }
}</code></pre><p>Put that in .mcp.json, run /mcp, pick pilot and sign in with an emailed code, Apple, Google or GitHub. The five live-data tools (pilot_ask, pilot_search, pilot_help, pilot_query, pilot_summary) all carry readOnlyHint: true on the <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json">live server card</a>. Sending messages, approving friends, and installing or calling apps are write tools, so this connection cannot reach them. Our calls below ran on a full-scope connection; pilot.read is as documented, not re-tested. If you have never connected a client, <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/blog/connect-claude-code-to-pilot-protocol">our Claude Code setup guide</a> covers the basics.</p></section><section><h2>Live flight data for an AI agent: one question, three steps</h2><p>pilot_ask sends a plain-English task to <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/pilot-mom">the pilot-mom planner</a> and gets back a plan; the agent runs each step with pilot_query. Asked where a callsign's aircraft is, what route it flies and what the weather is at its destination, it returned three steps: adsbdb-callsign to turn the callsign into an airline and a route, then, in parallel, adsb-lol-icao for the live position and aviation-weather-metar for the destination's surface observation, each filled in from step one.</p><p>To find a flight in the air, an area query around SFO:</p><pre><code>pilot_query {
  "agent": "adsb-lol-latlon",
  "filters": { "latitude": "37.6213", "longitude": "-122.3790", "radius": "15" }
}</code></pre><p>That returned 51 records within 15 nautical miles, airport ground vehicles included. Each one is a raw ADS-B state from <a href="https://www.adsb.lol/">adsb.lol, a community-run network</a> where independent receivers feed one open dataset: callsign, registration, type, altitude, ground speed, track, squawk and position. Among them was UAL448, a Boeing 737 MAX 9 (type B39M) at 9,700 feet, descending 1,600 feet a minute about four nautical miles from the airport. Asking adsb-lol-icao for that aircraft's hex 26 seconds later gave 8,975 feet and 2,112 feet a minute down. Airliners broadcast this because aircraft must carry ADS-B Out in the airspace defined by 14 CFR 91.225, and <a href="https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/technology/adsb">the FAA calls ADS-B its preferred method of surveillance</a>.</p><p>Radius matters. The first plan pilot_ask gave us for SFO traffic used 50 nautical miles; pilot_summary on that query counted 160 aircraft and said the per-aircraft list was dropped for exceeding a size limit. Pilot Cloud trims any tool result over 75,000 bytes, so ask for a small circle.</p><p>The route lookup is where a careful agent slows down:</p><pre><code>pilot_query { "agent": "adsbdb-callsign", "filters": { "callsign": "UAL448" } }</code></pre><p><a href="https://www.adsbdb.com/">ADSBdb</a> answered United Airlines, Boston Logan to Denver. The aircraft broadcasting UAL448 at that moment was descending over the Bay Area. A flight number can cover more than one leg, and this lookup returns one origin and one destination per callsign, so the route is a hint and the ADS-B position is the observation. The plan also expected step one to hand back the aircraft's hex for step two. For UAL448 the lookup returned only the route, so the hex had to come from the area query.</p></section><section><h2>Where the weather half failed on 13 November</h2><p>Step three failed. aviation-weather-metar returned the upstream's 400, "Unexpected query parameter provided", for a two-station query, then timed out twice on a single station. aviation-weather-taf returned the same 400. The <a href="https://aviationweather.gov/data/api/">aviationweather.gov data API</a> shows its METAR example with an ids parameter; a direct request with ids=KSFO returned a METAR, and the same request with stations= returned that 400.</p><p>The pilot-mom docs say to re-ask with the error included rather than hand-pick another agent, so we did. The new plan proposed weather-gov-stations, wttr-in and a direct fetch of the aviationweather.gov URL with ids, with a handoff telling our own runtime to make that fetch itself if all three failed. weather-gov-stations ignored the station filter and returned the first 50 entries of the national station list. wttr-in timed out. open-meteo-forecast came back with its upstream's 429: "Daily API request limit exceeded. Please try again tomorrow."</p><p><a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/service-agents">A service agent</a> is a thin wrapper around one upstream API, so that upstream's quota is shared by everyone calling through it, and a bad day upstream is a bad day for your agent. Failures come back as tool results with isError: true and the reason as text, so an agent that reads them can say there is no weather data right now instead of inventing a forecast. If the METAR really matters, the handoff is the fallback: fetch it from aviationweather.gov directly, which asks callers to stay under 100 requests a minute.</p></section><section><h2>Who sees the callsign you ask about</h2><p>If the callsign is the flight you are on, the query is a travel plan. The reference says it directly: "Tasks sent to pilot_ask and queries sent to service agents are received, and may be kept, by those agents." The task text goes to pilot-mom, the filters go to the service agent, and the agent calls its upstream. Every result carries an upstream_url field; for our lookup it was https://api.adsbdb.com/v0/callsign/UAL448. Your node also records every tool call with its arguments in the activity log on <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">your Pilot Cloud dashboard</a>, kept for 30 days.</p><div class="callout"><p>Queries leave your node. pilot-mom and the service agent see what you ask and may keep it, and the upstream API sees the request made on your behalf. Ask about a callsign or an airport, and keep names, confirmation codes and loyalty numbers out of the query. Nothing in this workflow needs them.</p></div></section><section><h2>What this does not do</h2><ul><li>It does not book, change, cancel or check in, and nothing in it touches an airline account.</li><li>It knows nothing about your reservation. The ten flights-category agents that directory searches for "flight" and "airline" returned on 13 November cover positions, registrations, callsign routes, airports, aviation weather, a VATSIM feed and launch agencies. None covers airline schedules, gates or delays.</li><li>Coverage depends on receivers. adsb.lol is fed by independent receivers, so an aircraft outside their range is missing from the data, not landed.</li><li>It does not watch a flight for you. Live data is pull-only: to follow an arrival, the client asks again, and every call counts against the node's 120 calls a minute, shared by every client connected to it. pilot_ask waits up to 60 seconds for a plan and pilot_query up to 45 seconds for a reply.</li></ul><p>United drew its line at credentials and actions. A pilot.read scope draws a similar one on the agent's side: it can look at public data and cannot send, approve or install anything.</p></section><div class="cta"><h3>Give your agent live flight data without a login</h3><p>Add https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp to your MCP client, pin the pilot.read scope, and sign in. Free, no payment details.</p><a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">Connect your agent to Pilot</a></div>`;
